#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# ordering_by

#% text
# `ordering_by(xs, f)` gives the positions of the elements of `xs` in the canonical order of `f(x)`; `ordering_by(xs, f, n)` the first `n` (the last `-n` for negative `n`, all of them for `all`). For an association it orders the values. `ordering_by(f)(xs)` is the operator form.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
ordering_by(["list", "of", "strings"], string_length)
#% output
#= [2, 1, 3]

#% cell
ordering_by(range(-5, 5), abs, 3)
#% output
#= [6, 5, 7]

#% cell
ordering_by(last)([[a, 2], [b, 1], [c, 7], [d, 5]])
#% output
#= [2, 1, 4, 3]

